# Match patterns # {#match-patterns}

A <dfn export>match pattern</dfn> is a [=string=] used to match URLs.

A match pattern is either the special value `<all_urls>`, or a string of the
form:

```
<scheme>://<host><path>
```

## Grammar ## {#match-pattern-grammar}

### <dfn for="match pattern" id="dfn-match-pattern-scheme">scheme</dfn> ### {#match-pattern-scheme}

|scheme| is one of a fixed list of literal scheme names, or `*`.

A pattern's |scheme| is the substring of the match pattern before the
first `://`.

The literal scheme values `http`, `https`, `file`, and `ftp`, plus a browser's own [=extension
scheme=], are valid |scheme| values in Chrome, Firefox, and Safari alike. `http`, `https`,
`file`, and `ftp` are four of the URL Standard's six [=special schemes=]; the other two, `ws`
and `wss`, come up in the divergences below. `data` is not a special scheme, which matters for
[[#all-urls]]: Firefox is the only one of the three browsers that includes `data` in
`<all_urls>`.

<div class=issue>
Beyond that common set, support diverges. Firefox additionally accepts `ws`, `wss`, and `data`.
Chrome additionally accepts `ws`/`wss` for `host_permissions` (but not for
`content_scripts.matches`) and its own `chrome://` WebUI scheme (policy/enterprise contexts
only). Safari accepts none of `ws`, `wss`, `data`, or a WebUI-equivalent scheme in a match
pattern at all.
</div>

If |scheme| is `*`, the pattern matches a fixed, implementation-defined
set of schemes in place of a literal scheme. That set always includes `http` and `https` in
Chrome, Firefox, and Safari alike.

Issue: Firefox's `*` scheme wildcard covers `ws` and `wss` in addition
to `http` and `https`. Chrome's and Safari's cover only `http` and
`https`.

### <dfn for="match pattern" id="dfn-match-pattern-host">host</dfn> ### {#match-pattern-host}

|host| is one of:

* `*`, matching any host.
* `*.` followed by a domain suffix (containing no `*` or `/`), matching
    that domain and any of its subdomains.
* a literal domain or IP-address literal (containing no `*` or `/`),
    matching only that exact host.

Those are the only shapes |host| takes when it contains a `*` at all; see
[[#match-pattern-parsing]] for what a `*` in any other position does to
parsing.

When |scheme| is `file`, |host| is omitted (the pattern has the form
`file://<path>` or `file:///<path>`, with `<path>` beginning at the third
slash regardless of what, if anything, appears between the second and
third slash).

Host comparison is case-insensitive.

Issue: Chrome and Safari match hosts case-insensitively. Firefox does
not. Firefox stores the pattern's host verbatim and compares it
byte-for-byte, so `*://EXAMPLE.com/*` matches nothing. Specifying
case-insensitive host matching is a behavior change for Firefox, not a
documentation fix.

Issue: Only Chrome canonicalizes a Unicode (non-ASCII) |host| to
Punycode at parse time. Firefox and Safari store the host verbatim. A
pattern host written with literal Unicode therefore matches nothing in
those two browsers, because the navigated URL's host is always
Punycode-encoded, and the author has to write the Punycode form
themselves.

A port is never written in |host|; see [[#match-pattern-parsing]] for what
writing one does to parsing.

Issue: Does a match pattern need a port component at all? Chrome's
grammar has one: a literal `<host>:<port>` matches only that port, and
omitting the port (the common case) matches any port. Safari's and
Firefox's grammars have none.

<div class="example">
`https://*.example.com/*` matches `https://example.com/`,
`https://www.example.com/`, and `https://a.b.example.com/`, but not
`https://notexample.com/`.
</div>

### <dfn for="match pattern" id="dfn-match-pattern-path">path</dfn> ### {#match-pattern-path}

|path| begins with `/`; see [[#match-pattern-parsing]] for what a
pattern with no |path| does to parsing.

<div class="example">
`http://example.com/` has a |path| of `/` and is a valid match pattern.
`http://example.com`, with no trailing slash and therefore no |path| at
all, [=parse a match pattern|fails to parse=].
</div>

|path| may contain any number of `*` characters, each matching zero or
more characters. No other character in |path| has special meaning; in
particular, unlike a [=glob=], `?` is an ordinary literal character in a
match pattern's |path|, not a single-character wildcard.

Path matching is case-sensitive in Chrome, Firefox, and Safari (Chrome exposes a
case-insensitive matching mode in its API, but no call site in the
browser's own permission or content-script matching code uses it).

Issue: Chrome and Firefox match the path component of a match pattern
against the URL's [=path=] and [=query=] string together (that is, against
`pathname + search`). Safari matches it against the [=path=] alone; the
[=query=] string is excluded. In
[w3c/webextensions#580](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/580#issuecomment-2070916942),
an Apple engineer stated in April 2024 that a bug was fixed in Safari
Technology Preview 192, without naming the behavior it concerned, so
that comment cannot be read either way as a statement about
query-string matching. The group needs to decide whether the path
component belongs in the specification as including the query string
and, if so, what change that means for Safari to match Chrome and
Firefox.

Issue: Percent-encoding is handled inconsistently. Chrome decodes
percent-encoded octets in both the URL and the pattern's |path| before
comparing (falling back to a raw comparison for byte sequences that
aren't valid UTF-8). Firefox and Safari perform no decoding at all: a
pattern containing a literal, unescaped reserved character will not
match a URL where the user agent encoded that same character, in either
browser. See also
[w3c/webextensions#945](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/945),
a related but distinct percent-encoding discussion scoped to
declarativeNetRequest's `urlFilter` syntax rather than match patterns.

<div class="example">
`https://example.com/foo/*` matches `https://example.com/foo/`,
`https://example.com/foo/bar`, and `https://example.com/foo/bar?baz`,
but not `https://example.com/foobar` or `https://example.com/foo`
(without a trailing slash).
</div>

## <dfn export>`<all_urls>`</dfn> ## {#all-urls}

The special match pattern `<all_urls>` matches all URLs whose [=scheme=] is
in a permitted set of schemes, regardless of [=url/host=] or [=path=]. `http` and `https` are in that set
in every engine, in every context that defines one.

<div class=issue>
Beyond `http` and `https`, the permitted scheme set for `<all_urls>` is the largest three-way
divergence in this area, and it is not merely a different literal list;
the *shape* of the set differs.

* Chrome's set depends on which manifest surface is asking. For
    `content_scripts.matches`, `<all_urls>` covers `http`, `https`,
    `file`, `ftp`, and the isolated-web-app UUID scheme (plus `chrome://`
    WebUI pages, gated behind an experimental flag). For
    `host_permissions`, the set additionally includes `ws` and `wss`.
    Neither surface includes `data:` or its own [=extension scheme=].
* Firefox uses one fixed set for every surface: `http`, `https`, `ws`,
    `wss`, `file`, `ftp`, and `data`. This is the only one of the three
    browsers that includes `data:` in `<all_urls>`.
* Safari's set is the narrowest by far: `http`, `https`, and its own
    [=extension scheme=]. `file:` is matched only if the caller has
    separately opted in to allowing file access; by default it is
    excluded. `ftp`, `ws`, `wss`, and `data` are never included.

The group needs to choose a portable `<all_urls>` scheme set (or
explicitly leave `file:` and other non-http(s) schemes as a
browser-specific opt-in, matching Safari's model, rather than an
unconditional inclusion as in Firefox's model).
</div>

<div class="example">
Given a `content_scripts.matches` list of `["<all_urls>"]`:

<table class=simple>
<thead>
<tr><th>URL<th>Chrome<th>Firefox<th>Safari
<tbody>
<tr><td>`https://example.com/`<td>runs<td>runs<td>runs
<tr><td>`ftp://example.com/`<td>runs<td>runs<td>does not run
<tr><td>`data:text/html,...`<td>does not run<td>runs<td>does not run
<tr><td>`file:///home/user/x.html`<td>runs<td>runs<td>runs only if file access has separately been granted
</table>
</div>

## Parsing a match pattern ## {#match-pattern-parsing}

To <dfn>parse a match pattern</dfn> given |input|:

1. If |input| is `<all_urls>`, return `<all_urls>`.
1. If |input| does not contain `://`, return failure.
1. Let |scheme| be the substring of |input| before the first `://`.
1. If |scheme| is not `*`, and is not one of the permitted literal
    scheme values (see [[#match-pattern-scheme]]), return failure.
1. Let |rest| be the substring of |input| following that `://`.
1. If |scheme| is `file`:
    1. If |rest| contains no U+002F (/), return failure.
    1. Let |host| be the empty string. Any text of |rest| before its
        first U+002F (/) is discarded, not read as a host.
    1. Let |path| be the substring of |rest| starting at its first
        U+002F (/), inclusive.
1. Otherwise:
    1. If |rest| does not contain U+002F (/), return failure.
    1. Let |host| be the substring of |rest| before its first U+002F (/).
    1. If |host| contains U+003A (:), return failure.
    1. If |host| is not `*`, |host| does not consist of `*.` followed
        by one or more characters containing no U+002A (*), and |host|
        contains U+002A (*), return failure.
    1. Let |path| be the substring of |rest| starting at its first
        U+002F (/), inclusive.
1. Return a [=match pattern=] whose |scheme| is |scheme|, whose |host|
    is |host|, and whose |path| is |path|.

A `file` match pattern's |host| is not merely omitted from the input, it
is never read as a host at all: whatever text appears between `file://`
and the next `/`, if any, is discarded rather than validated. This
matches the [[#match-pattern-host|existing description]] of `file`
match patterns. It is a separate question from why the
[[#match-pattern-matching-algorithm]] below skips |path| comparison for
a `file` |pattern|, which this algorithm does not change.

<div class="example">
`<all_urls>` and `https://*.example.com/*` both parse successfully.
`example.com/*` (no `://`) and `https://example.com` (no |path|) both
fail to parse. `http://example.com:8080/*` also fails to parse; see
[[#match-pattern-host]] for the open question of whether match
patterns need a port component at all.
</div>

## Matching algorithm ## {#match-pattern-matching-algorithm}

|pattern| here is either `<all_urls>` or the result of a successful
[=parse a match pattern|parse=] of a match pattern string; see
[[#match-pattern-parsing]].

To determine whether a [=match pattern=] |pattern| matches a URL |url|:

1. Let |url record| be the result of [=basic URL parser|parsing=] |url|.
1. If |pattern| is `<all_urls>`:
    1. If the [=scheme=] of |url record| is in the permitted scheme set
        for `<all_urls>` (see above), return true.
    1. Otherwise, return false.
1. If the [=scheme=] of |url record| does not match |pattern|'s
    [=match pattern/scheme=] (a literal scheme must be equal; `*` must
    be one of the schemes it expands to for the current context),
    return false.
1. If the [=url/host=] of |url record| does not match |pattern|'s
    [=match pattern/host=], return false.
1. If |pattern|'s [=match pattern/scheme=] is not `file`, and
    |pattern|'s [=match pattern/path=] does not match the [=path=] of
    |url record| (see the path matching notes above for what "path"
    includes), return false.
1. Return true.

This algorithm is run against the URL produced by
[Determine the URL for matching a document](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#determine-the-url-for-matching-a-document) when matching a document
for `content_scripts`, not necessarily against the document's literal
URL; it does not itself define how the URL of a document with an
[=opaque origin=] is resolved.

<div class=issue>
Firefox additionally supports a stricter "explicit" matching mode (used for
`host_permissions`-style checks as opposed to `content_scripts.matches`-
style checks) in which a pattern that matches subdomains (a leading `*.`
or bare `*` host) never matches, regardless of the URL. Neither Chrome's
nor Safari's match-pattern matching code has an equivalent mode, and
neither engine's permission-checking code has one either: Chrome's
permission code separates host patterns only by which manifest key
declared them, not by whether they contain a subdomain wildcard, and
Safari's closest analogous distinction (a pattern naming a specific host
versus a pattern that matches every host) is a coarser split that still
treats a subdomain-wildcard pattern the same as a literal one.

The group needs to decide whether this "explicit host" restriction belongs
in this section as a named concept, or is purely a permission-UI concern
out of scope for match pattern matching itself.
</div>

<div class="example">
Given the pattern `*://*.example.com/*`:

<table class=simple>
<thead>
<tr><th>URL<th>Chrome<th>Firefox<th>Safari
<tbody>
<tr><td>`https://www.example.com/`<td>matches<td>matches<td>matches
<tr><td>`http://example.com/`<td>matches<td>matches<td>matches
<tr><td>`wss://example.com/`<td>does not match<td>matches<td>does not match
</table>

Firefox's `*` scheme wildcard additionally covers `ws`/`wss`.
</div>

## Unparseable match patterns ## {#unparseable-match-patterns}

A match pattern string that does not [=parse a match pattern|parse=]
does not match any URL.

<div class=issue>
What a user agent does beyond that, on encountering such a string in a
manifest, depends on which manifest key contains it, and differs by
browser.

For a permission-bearing key (`permissions`, `host_permissions`,
`optional_permissions`, `optional_host_permissions`), Chrome, Firefox,
and Safari alike drop the one unparseable entry and load the extension
with the rest of the list; Chrome and Firefox additionally record a
warning, Safari does not.

For `content_scripts.matches`, Chrome and Firefox instead refuse to
load the extension at all when any one entry fails to parse. Safari
drops only the unparseable entry (or, if every entry in one content
script definition fails, that definition) and still loads the
extension.

The group needs to decide whether the consequence of an unparseable
match pattern needs to be uniform across keys, and if so, which of the
above.
</div>
